**Correctness: 12/12 PASS** with max_rel 3e-6 to 6e-6 ≪ 2e-2 — the HIP chunked kernel is validated against the oracle including H=128 S=512 D=128, plus the nonzero-initial-state contract vs the cooperative kernel.

**A/B results** (S=512, D=128):
| H | coop | chunked | speedup |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | 1895.7 | 1134.6 | 1.67× |
| 8 | 1972.2 | 1151.5 | 1.71× |
| 16 | 2892.1 | 1158.5 | 2.50× |
| 32 | 2919.7 | 1571.6 | 1.86× |
| 64 | 2930.0 | 2516.5 | 1.16× |
| 128 | 3198.3 | 4127.7 | **0.77× (regression!)** |
| S=1024 H=32 | 5859 | 2701.6 | 2.17× |
| S=2048 H=32 | 11929.7 | 5047.6 | 2.36× |

Interesting pattern: **chunked wins big at low-to-mid H and long S (1.7-2.5×), but REGRESSES at H=128 (0.77×)**. The chunked time grows with H (1134 → 4127µs), i.e., it's throughput-bound at high H — expected, since the chunked path does ~2-3× the FLOPs (grams + inverse + extra GEMMs) and at H=128 the machine is saturated by work, not latency.

Why does the chunked time grow so steeply with H (1134 → 4127, 3.6×) when coop only grows 1895 → 3198 (1.7×)? At H=128: B·H·nc = 1024 K2 blocks (chunk-local) + 512 K3 blocks. The K2 kernel is the FLOP-heavy one (grams 2×cs²/2×D with exp, inverse, 5 GEMMs of cs²D). Let me estimate K2's cost: grams per chunk: 2×2080×128 = 532K FMA + 532K exp (SFU-bound!). exp throughput on gfx942: SFU rate = 16/cycle/CU? (1/4 of FP32). 532K exps / (16/cycle) = 33K cycles ≈ 20µs per chunk-block?? No wait — that's per block if ONE CU ran it: 532K exps / (64 lanes... SFU: MUFU 16/cycle... per CU per cycle ~16 exps → 532K/16 = 33K cycles = 19µs @1.7GHz. Hmm that seems way too slow. But blocks are spread across 228 CUs — at H=1, nc=8: 8 blocks → 8 CUs busy, K2 wall = ~19-25µs. At H=128: 1024 blocks / 228 CUs = 4.5 waves × ~20µs ≈ 90µs... 

Hmm but the total chunked time at H=1 is 1134µs — way more than my estimate (K2 ~20µs + K3 ~8×12µs ≈ 100µs + K1 trivial → ~150µs expected, got 1134µs!). So my performance model is way off — something is much slower than estimated. At H=1: what dominates? Let me think:
- K3: 8 chunks × (u: 2048 outputs×128; o: same; C: 4096×64) per (bh, rowblock=4 blocks): per block per chunk: u 2048/256=8 outputs × 128 = 1024 FMA + o 1024 + C 16 outputs×64 = 1024 → 3K FMA/thread + LDS/GMEM traffic. With 4 blocks → per-chunk serial ~3K FMA/thread ≈ 12K cycles ≈ 7µs?? × 8 chunks = 56µs.
- K2: grams ~8.1 pairs/thread × 128 × (4 loads + exp + 2FMA) — the exp: 1024 exps/thread; if MUFU throughput is 1/4 per lane per cycle → 4096 cycles; loads: kj/kt/Lj/Lt from GMEM (L2) — 4×128×8 = 4096 loads per thread! At ~4 cycles per L2 load (latency hidden by parallelism?) — hmm, but these are STRIDED (each thread reads 4 different rows, sequential within the row). Coalescing: threads in a warp process DIFFERENT pairs → each thread reads different rows simultaneously → the warp's 32 lanes hit 32 different rows at the same k → stride D*4B=512B apart → TERRIBLE coalescing (each lane = separate 128B sector). That's the problem! The gram phase reads 32×4 sectors per iteration...

That explains the slowness. Fix options:
(a) Swap loop nesting: thread-per-k-dim × warp-reduce per pair: each warp handles one (j,t) pair, lanes iterate k (coalesced 32×4B = 128B per warp-load ✓), then shfl-reduce. 8 warps → 8 pairs per pass; 2080 pairs / 8 = 260 passes × (128/32 = 4 loads × 4 arrays + exp×4 + reduce ~5 shfl) ≈ 260×(16 loads + 16 exp + 10) ≈ heavy too but COALESCED.
(b) Stage k, q, L tiles in LDS first: k,q: cs×D×4B = 32KB each — too big with sM+sN (16+16KB)... For D=128: k(32KB)+q(32KB)+L(32KB) = 96KB — NO. Stage only k and L (q streamed): 64KB + sM/sN 32KB = 96KB — no.
